sulfates fatty alcohols silicones  preservatives why are they in shampoo  and conditioner and do you need to avoid  them hi i'm michelle chemistry phd and  cosmetic chemist today we're doing a  deep dive into some of the key  ingredients in hair care products this  video was sponsored by the ordinary who  turned a lot of people into skincare  nerds with their focus on ingredients  and now they're moving into hair care  let's start with shampoo to me the best  way to approach analyzing formulas is to  think what is the point of this product  what is its function shampoo obviously  cleans your hair and scalp just like  skin your scalp produces sebum which we  usually think of as oil this oil keeps  your skin and scalp nice and soft and it  also travels along the hair to keep that  soft as well but sometimes we have too  much oil some of us like me more than  others it also tends to stick to things  like dirt dust skin flakes and pollution  you might also use styling products that  need to be washed off the problem is if  you just try to use water to degrasify  everything a bit it usually doesn't work  very well because oil and water don't  mix so to make them mix we use a shampoo  and the key ingredients in the shampoo  that clean are surfactants surfactants  are really useful ingredients because  they have a head that likes water and a  tail that likes oil and so instead of  repelling each other the surfactants  allow the oil to basically be smuggled  into the water in lots of tiny droplets  and then you can wash that away down the  drain there are surfactants in tons of  different products because getting oil  and water to stay together is really  useful for cleaning the best surfactants  are anionic surfactants these have a  negatively charged head and they're used  in tons of cleaning products shampoo  obviously but also laundry detergent  dishwashing detergent body washes and  face cleansers some of the anionic  surfaces that you might have seen in  your shampoos and cleanses are sulfates  sarcoisonates torays and isothionates  traditional soaps also have anionic  surfactants called carboxylates so  that's what the sulfates are doing but  why are people talking about  sulfate-free well a lot of it is based  on misinformation once you start digging  into the science behind shampoos you  start to realize that limiting yourself  to self-a-free shampoos  doesn't really make sense sulfate  surfactants are a diverse group of  ingredients that behave in different  ways and on top of that they can be  combined in formulas in lots of  different ways to give very different  shampoos it's a bit like eggs in a  recipe just seeing eggs in the  ingredients list doesn't tell you  whether they use the whites or the yolks  or a mix how the food tastes or whether  you'll like it  unless you have an egg allergy in which  case you probably should avoid it unless  you really like pain or you're vegan so  generalizing cell phase and products  doesn't really work it isn't like  shampoos with sulfates are always  harsher or more irritating or strip  color faster than sulfate-free shampoos  there is a lot of overlap sulfates are  some of the most commonly used anionic  surfactants in personal care products  the two you'll see most in shampoos are  sodium lauryl sulfate sls and sodium  laureth sulfate sles these sound pretty  similar but they're actually very  different sodium lauryl sulfate is the  more famous one and it has a bad  reputation which has now spread to other  sulfates it has a narrow structure and  that means it can potentially absorb  into skin more easily and keep  irritating for longer if sls is in a  formula on its own in a high  concentration then it will probably be  quite irritating and this is one of the  big reasons why it has this irritant  reputation a lot of the time in  peer-reviewed studies and in dermatology  patch testing sls is used by itself to  make sure it is the specific ingredient  that someone is reacting to but this  also means it isn't in its usual  environment which is in a product with a  lot of other ingredients that change how  it interacts with skin and with hair  there are lots of formulating tricks  that are usually used in sls products  here are some of them creating mixed  micelles by combining sls with other  surfactants this makes sls less likely  to wander off on its own into your skin  or hair  it's like peer pressure but for  molecules adding polymers that act as a  raft to hold onto free sls molecules  again this is so it can't run away into  your skin or hair adding skin protecting  ingredients like humectants and  antioxidants that can prevent or reverse  some of the irritation now sles  sodium laureth sulfate actually isn't  one single ingredient it's actually a  category of ingredients that are all  listed under the same name in an  ingredients list ingredient lists don't  really lie but they don't really tell  you the full story either sles is  ethoxylated which means it's sls but  with a bunch of ethoxy units inserted  next to the sulfur like this the number  of units can change and this changes how  the specific type of sles interacts with  everything else water oil or other  surfactant molecules having these ethoxy  units means that less sles will wander  off on its own into your skin compared  to sls ethoxylation also makes the  molecule bigger which means it doesn't  penetrate skin as easily and is more  gentle it's a bit like walking through a  crowd if you want to get through a crowd  faster you try to hunch and make  yourself smaller you don't spread your  arms out there's one study that found  that sles was gentler than sls which is  not very surprising but it was also  gentler than a whole bunch of  non-surface effectors that you often  find in sulfate-free products and on top  of this formulators can use the same  formulating tricks that i mentioned  before to make sles products even  gentler now with color fading  i have a strong personal interest in  this as you can probably tell when i  first dyed my hair four years ago i use  sulfate-free shampoos because that's  what everyone says is best for keeping  your hair color for longer except  i hated all the selfie-free shampoos  that i tried and i spent a lot of money  on them because i went with a lot of  very fancy brands i ended up just going  back to sulphate shampoos and i didn't  actually notice much of a difference  with my hair color fading recently i got  to see some experimental data on color  fading that confirmed what i noticed  happening with my hair with color fading  there are some differences between  different shampoo formulas as you'd  expect from everything i've said so far  there is a lot of overlap between self a  full and sulfate free shampoos the ph of  the shampoo actually made a bigger  difference than whether or not it had  sulfates higher ph more alkaline  shampoos will leech some dyes out faster  but by far the biggest thing that made a  difference was just water just washing  coloured hair in water without shampoo  leeches out a ton of dye the differences  between different shampoos are tiny  compared to washing versus not washing  so my problem was that a lot of  sulfate-free shampoos were formulated to  be too gentle to clean my scalp and hair  properly i have a pretty oily scalp and  so that meant that i either wash my hair  every day or i just suffered with sticky  limp hair and an itchy scalp and  sometimes that happen even if i wash  daily using a shampoo with sulfates let  me wash my hair less often and so i  actually ended up with less fading  sulfate's also quite cheap which a lot  of people paint as a bad thing that  companies are just using cheap  ingredients to save money even though  there might be better more expensive  options and this does happen but it's  never the only reason formulators use a  particular ingredient number one you  have to make a product that works the  cheapest ingredient is just water but if  a company tried to sell plain water as a  product it would flop really hard number  two price doesn't directly match with  quality i'm sure we all have a favorite  cheap meal that we'd happily pick over a  bunch of more expensive options and  shout out to this video sponsor the  ordinary most of us have probably tried  some super budget-friendly skincare  products from the ordinary that worked  better than more expensive ones and  sulfates do actually have some really  good properties that make them great in  cleansing products on top of just the  price of the raw material one of the  reasons sulfates work out to be cheap is  because they're multifunctional  obviously they clean really well but  they also foam well more foam doesn't  actually mean a better clean but  psychologically if there isn't that much  foam most people just don't think it's  doing a good job sulfates also help  create a thicker texture and you can  usually add a bit of salt to make a  sulfate product thicken even more  shampoos need to be a little bit thick  so it sits nicely in your hand and you  can get it onto your hair effectively  it also makes it feel more concentrated  a lot of other surfactants don't have  these properties so if you take out the  sulfates you often have to add in a  bunch of other ingredients to boost the  foam and make it thicker which adds to  the cost let's take a look at the  ordinary sulfate 4 cleanser for body and  hair which is a pretty stripped back  shampoo formula sodium laureth sulfate  is the anionic surfactant it's at four  percent which you can hopefully tell  from the name this is pretty low for  shampoo but this also doubles up as a  body wash which normally has lower  amounts so this keeps it gentle  specifically they're using sles2 which  means there's two ethoxy units per  molecule this is the most common type  used in shampoos because it gives a good  balance between cleaning gentleness and  cost apart from the anionic surfactant  we have some other ingredients water is  the first ingredient and that's pretty  important it keeps the surfactants  spread out the right way when you put  them on your hair or skin if it's too  concentrated it's really harsh you do  not want 100 surfactant on your skin or  your hair trust me getting the right  dilution also affects the thickness of  the shampoo polysorbate 20 is another  surfactant it's not as good at cleaning  and it's usually used as a solubilizer  so it might be in there to help the  chocolate dissolve xanthan gum is a  thickener that makes the texture thicker  tocopherol is vitamin e which is an  antioxidant there's a mix of  preservatives which stops the bacteria  mold from having a fun multiplying  festival inside the shampoo you need a  preservative whenever you have a  water-based formula there are chelating  agents which grab onto metal ions metal  lines can make anionic surfactants work  worse especially when there's hard water  chelating agents also help preservatives  work better like i said this is a  relatively simple formula so in other  shampoos you'll have other ingredients  too you might have other surfactants  because combining multiple surfactants  can make a cleanser more gentle some of  them can also boost foam or moisturize  skin shampoos also often have detangling  ingredients because when hair is wet the  cuticle sticks up more so the surface is  rougher and it can snag on other hairs  more easily so most shampoos will have  some ingredients that very thinly coat  the hair to make a smoothing layer the  most common ingredients for these are  polyquaterniums sometimes you'll also  see colorants fragrances and pearlizing  agents to make the shampoo look and  smell nicer my scalp gets dry and  irritated from a lot of shampoos so i  was pretty curious to see how this very  simple formula would go for me this  turned out to be really good for my  scalp one of the better tolerated  shampoos i've used having multiple  surfactants is usually good because it  reduces irritation but they've gotten  around this by using a low percentage of  a gentle type of sles there also isn't  any fragrance in this i personally like  a bit of fragrance in my hair products  but i know a lot of people want or need  to avoid it it's actually pretty hard to  find fragrance free hair products so i'm  really happy to see this available as an  option now onto hair conditioners hair  conditioners have a few more functions  than shampoo and different people use  them for different purposes if you have  short hair you might not actually need  hair conditioner conditioners can add  shine smooth over damage and reduce  tangling and static to do this they  leave a very thin coating on hair  strands there is this myth that cheap  conditioners will coat hair but  expensive ones don't this is not true  you need a coating because your hair is  dead and as it grows it accumulates  damage and ends up looking chipped like  this hair conditioner smooth over this  roughness by plugging in the gaps so  hair doesn't snag and cause more of  these bits to break off the main  ingredients used in hair conditioners  are cationic surfactants these are a lot  like the anionic surfactants we saw in  shampoos except they have a positively  charged head when hair is wet it gets a  negative charge and the damaged bits of  hair usually have a stronger negative  charge opposite charges attract and so  these positive heads in the cationic  surfactants help them stick to the  negatively charged hair the positive  charges are also handy because  once you have enough attached the hair  becomes slightly positive which will  actually repel extra cationic  surfactants what this means is you end  up with a thinner more even film that  feels silky rather than the sort of film  that you might get with a lot of oils  like your natural hair oil that's heavy  and weighs hair down the most common  type of cationic surfactant that you'll  see in conditioners are quaternary  ammonium ions nicknamed quartz you'll  see ownium in a lot of their names the  ordinary's conditioner has two percent  behendramonium chloride in conditioners  these cationic surfactants are usually  combined with fatty alcohols the most  common fatty alcohol is saterial alcohol  which is a mix of settle and sterile  alcohol these fatty alcohols make the  conditioner thicker so it's easier to  apply in your hair more importantly it  also combines with these cationic  surfactants to make an ordered  arrangement called a lamellar structure  this is a bit like a pile of flat tiles  that can slide over each other and coat  the hair these main ingredients are also  why a lot of people get breakouts on  their back with conditioner cationic  surfactants are quite irritating and  fatty alcohols are pretty good at  clogging pores so if you find you get a  lot of back acne make sure you scrub  your back properly after using  conditioner the ordinary's conditioner  is again pretty simple they've only got  seven ingredients so there's the  cationic surfactant and the fatty  alcohol which are the standard  ingredients in every hair conditioner  that form that thin conditioning film we  also have water and just like in  shampoos it's there to make sure  everything is at the right concentration  and thickness for putting on your wet  hair there's also gua gum which thickens  up the formula more and of course  there's preservatives to control  bacteria and mold with more complex  conditioners you might have extra  ingredients and these change the sorts  of coatings left on the hair silicones  are really common and there is a huge  range of these some tend to be heavier  and people get the impression that  silicones always weigh hair down but  this is not a blanket rule some  silicones like my favorite  amodimethicone are charged and so just  like the cationic surfactant you only  get a thin film and any excess stuff  actually gets repelled there are other  polymers like polyquaternium similar to  what's used in shampoos to detangle and  add volume you might see natural oils  like coconut and argan oil these tend to  form thicker films on hair and this  really weighs it down and so usually  they're used at low concentrations and  conditioners you might also have  hydrolyzed proteins and amino acids  these can penetrate into the hair a bit  and add strength by plugging holes in  the hair i was actually quite surprised  by how well this worked on my hair if  you followed my hair bleaching journey  you'll know that i pretty much need  specialized silicones like emo  dimethicone to make my hair not be straw  this conditioner detangled my hair  really nicely when i was washing it and  it felt nice for about a day  but by day two my hair started feeling a  bit dry i think this would have been  fantastic for my hair before i bleached  it but now i need those extra damaged  coating ingredients so i would recommend  trying this if your hair is not quite  this level of damaged if you want a  conditioner that doesn't weigh your hair  down or if you want something fragrance  free again there really aren't that many  options for fragrance-free hair  conditioners so i'm really happy to see  this as an option for people who want it  i hope you enjoyed this deep dive into  shampoos and conditioners let me know if  you're interested in other product  breakdowns make sure you like and  subscribe links to the ordinary's hair  products are in the description more  videos about cosmetic science are  popping up around me now

